WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Charles Kingsford Smith: Australia's Aviation
Hero

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.499
This episode is made possible byt the realistic
online game WAR THUNDER.

00:00:03.499 --> 00:00:06.060
Check out this game through the link in the
description below!

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.490
Go through that our link and you not only
support this show but you you get a free premium

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:13.049
tank or aircraft and three days of premium
time as a bonus!

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:20.380
And let's get into it...

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.619
During the late 19th century, a German man
named Otto Lilienthal achieved some of the

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:27.690
first successful, repeated flights using gliders.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:32.719
Regrettably, he suffered a crash on August
9, 1896, and died the next day.

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:37.149
Reportedly, his last words were "Sacrifices
must be made!"

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:40.650
This was a mentality shared by all the pioneers
of flight.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.579
During the inception of aviation, there were
few professions out there more dangerous.

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:49.699
A quick look at all the pilots from the first
decades of flight and you will find very few

00:00:49.699 --> 00:00:53.590
of them who got to see retirement after a
long and successful career.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:58.280
If you'll pardon the cliché, these men and
women were made of sterner stuff than the

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.280
rest.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.359
One of these daredevils was Australian Charles
Kingsford Smith.

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:06.210
He discovered his passion for flying during
World War I and, for the rest of his life,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.480
always looked for new opportunities to soar
through the skies.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.750
In the process, he set a number of world firsts
such as the first trans-Pacific flight from

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.950
the United States to Australia.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.540
Unfortunately, like many of his peers, Kingsford
Smith's career ended in tragedy and his final

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.920
resting place remains, to this day, a mystery.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:33.220
Early Days
Born on February 9, 1897, in Hamilton, a suburb

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.560
of Brisbane, Australia, Charles Edward Kingsford
Smith was the seventh child of a banker named

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.240
William Charles Smith and Catherine Mary,
née Kingsford.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.130
Although the family originally went simply
by Smith, they added the surname Kingsford

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.920
in 1903 when they relocated to Canada.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.770
The move did not last long and, in 1907, the
Kingsford Smiths were back in Australia, but

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.110
this time in Sydney.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.539
Charles became known as Smithy, a nickname
he would carry for the rest of his life.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:03.360
When he was nine years old, Charles almost
drowned at Bondi Beach when he got dragged

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.910
out to sea by a rip current.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.610
The young boy struggled to stay afloat, but
eventually lost consciousness.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.110
He was saved just in time by the lifeguard
service which had only recently came into

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.850
existence, and was resuscitated by a nurse
who was in the right place at the right time.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.510
Later, during his aviation career, people
said that Kingsford Smith was terrified of

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.590
flying over the ocean and would get sick whenever
he did it.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.900
This may have been the genesis of his angst.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.840
As a teenager, Charles enrolled at a technical
school where he studied to become an engineer.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.100
He also joined the Senior Cadets, a military
youth organization.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.170
Service in the War
Image suggestion: Sapper in World War I

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.910
After the outbreak of World War I, Kingsford
Smith enlisted in the Australian Imperial

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.470
Force in February 1915.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.900
The 18-year-old took part in the Gallipoli
campaign where he served as a sapper with

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.650
the 4th Signal Troop, 2nd Division Signal
Company.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.200
He had his second brush with death here.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.220
As he described in a letter to his parents,
he heard some "ping" noises very close to

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.220
him.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.710
When he finally reached shelter, Kingsford
Smith noticed that a bullet came so close

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.610
to his head that it had frayed the edge of
his cap.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:08.000
Later, he became a dispatch rider during campaigns
in Egypt and France.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.430
After being promoted to sergeant, Smithy transferred
to the Australian Flying Corps in October

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.880
1916.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.870
This is where he got his first taste of flying
and developed a passion that would follow

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.400
him for the rest of his life.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.710
He trained in England where he showed immediate
aptitude as a pilot.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:27.280
He was commissioned as second lieutenant with
the Royal Flying Corps (RFC), the precursor

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.620
to the Royal Air Force.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.620
Kingsford Smith was sent to the French front
as part of the No. 23 Squadron.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.150
There, he shot down four enemy aircraft, as
well as launching multiple attacks on ground

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.450
targets and balloons.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.380
Inevitably, Smithy was bested in a dogfight
and was shot down himself.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:46.810
He walked away from the crash, although his
injuries did require amputation of several

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:47.810
toes.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:52.030
Kingsford Smith received the Military Cross
"for conspicuous gallantry and devotion to

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.030
duty".

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.150
Because his recuperation would take a long
time, he was allowed to visit his parents

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:02.280
and Australia and, upon returning, was promoted
to Captain and assigned as a flying instructor.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:09.910
Life after the War
By the time Smithy was fully healed, the war

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:10.910
had ended.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:16.200
He wasn't sure what he wanted to do with his
life, but he knew that it had to involve flying.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.870
He teamed up with another pilot named Cyril
Maddocks and formed a joy-flight company that

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.950
offered pleasure airplane trips throughout
England.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:27.540
However, the young Smith was already looking
for opportunities that would bring him fame

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.540
and fortune.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:32.900
In 1919, Australian Prime Minister Billy Hughes
announced the Great Air Race.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.680
A prize of £10,000 would be awarded to the
first person who completed a flight from Great

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.600
Britain to Australia in under 30 days.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.760
Unsurprisingly, Kingsford Smith wanted to
take part in the race, but he was denied membership

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.500
into the Royal Aero Club of Australia (RACA),
and, by default, entry into the competition.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.960
Officials were concerned over Smithy's lack
of navigational experience over the 11,180-mile

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.050
route and thought that it would be dangerous
to let him race.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:04.380
Instead, the prize was claimed by brothers
Sirs Ross and Keith Macpherson Smith.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.430
Although he was not allowed to compete in
the Great Air Race, an idea sprouted in Smithy's

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.430
mind.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.100
It was another surefire way to glory: the
first trans-Pacific flight from the United

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.960
States to Australia.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.910
Spoiler alert: this would be the feat that
brought him everlasting fame, but now was

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.120
not the time.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.710
Kingsford Smith traveled to America to search
for sponsors for his daring escapade but failed

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:27.710
to find any.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.930
He occupied his time with other flying gigs
which included stunt work for a circus and

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:32.930
for Hollywood movies.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:38.020
However, he saw another pilot die in an accident
and decided that the job was too risky, even

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.020
for him.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.300
He returned to Australia in 1921.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:49.680
Back Home
He might have had it with life as a stunt

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.890
pilot, but the experience did not detract
from Smithy's pleasure of flying.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:56.130
Back home, he started another joy-riding company.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.680
He also became a salaried pilot with the West
Australian Airways.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.060
Started by another RFC veteran, Sir Norman
Brearley, this was the first Australian airline

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.560
with a scheduled air service.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:10.060
In his personal life, Smithy married Thelma
Eileen Hope Corboy in 1923.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:14.480
By all accounts, it was an unhappy marriage
that only lasted for a few years as Kingsford

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.620
Smith was not made for a stable life in one
place.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:22.300
His biographer Ian Mackersey said that the
aviator's mind was always in the sky, thinking

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.380
about the next time when he would be able
to fly again.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.889
When he was on the ground, Kingsford Smith
enjoyed women, booze, and spending his money

00:06:28.889 --> 00:06:32.370
almost as fast as he made it.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:39.240
The Trans-Pacific Flight
Smithy believed there was huge potential for

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.080
an airmail service in Australia.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.860
Together with another aviator called Charles
Ulm, he started the Interstate Flying Services,

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.570
but found it difficult securing contracts.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:52.389
To show people that airmail was the future,
the two pilots wanted to perform some demonstration

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:54.710
flights to attract sponsors and investors.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.960
They did a round trip of Australia in a little
over 10 days which garnered plenty of attention.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.830
Sensing an opportunity to secure funding,
Kingsford Smith forgot all about his airmail

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:08.840
business and, instead, once again promoted
his true dream: the trans-Pacific flight.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.050
This time, it worked.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.440
Kingsford Smith not only obtained a grant
from the government of New South Wales, but

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:19.480
also received financial backing from two businessmen:
Myer Department Store founder Sydney Myer

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.490
and California oil tycoon George Allan Hancock.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.980
Image suggestion: The Southern Cross
First off, they had to buy a plane.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.650
Smithy and Ulm traveled all the way to America
to find the one they wanted but, bizarrely,

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.389
still ended up buying one from another Australian.

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:36.270
Specifically, they purchased a Fokker F.VII
from polar explorer Sir Hubert Wilkins.

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:40.620
It started life as the Detroiter, but Smith
rechristened it the Southern Cross.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.510
The goal was to take off from Oakland, California,
and finish in Australia.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:49.290
This was not a non-stop flight, so they would
land to refuel two times, the first of which

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:50.720
would be in Hawaii.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.590
Over the previous year, ten pilots had died
attempting just this leg of the trip.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.070
The odds were not in their favor, but the
airmen were determined to carry on.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:00.070
Image suggestion: The flight team
https://discoveringanzacs.naa.gov.au/browse/gallery/13673

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:01.380
Kingsford Smith had a four-man team.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.160
He was the pilot while Ulm acted as relief
pilot.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.880
On board were also two Americans: James Warner
who was the radio operator and Harry Lyon

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.290
who served as navigator.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.990
The foursome took off from Oakland on May
31, 1928.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:18.050
They reached the island of Kauai to refuel
after an uneventful 27 hours.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:21.560
From there, they set off on the longest stage
of their journey to Fiji.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:25.110
To make matters worse, they encountered a
massive lightning storm but, nevertheless,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.070
reached the island of Suva in one piece.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:32.180
The final stage saw them fly into Brisbane
Airport to a hero's welcome as tens of thousands

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:33.599
of people cheered them on.

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:38.480
After 83 hours and 38 minutes of flying time,
Kingsford Smith and his crew completed the

00:08:38.480 --> 00:09:10.230
first ever trans-Pacific flight.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:10:35.770
Across the Tasman Sea
Kingsford Smith received a ton of rewards

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:40.060
for his feat, including the Air Force Cross,
an honorary position as squadron leader with

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:43.730
the Royal Australian Air Force and over £20,000
in funding.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:47.450
Now that the trans-Pacific flight was out
of the way, he could focus again on his more

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:50.440
practical ambition – that of establishing
an airmail company.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.010
Like before, he considered a series of daring
flights to be the best way of showcasing the

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:53.010
viability of such a service.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.770
In August 1928, just a few months after his
journey across the Pacific, Smithy performed

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.790
the first non-stop flight across mainland
Australia, taking off from Point Cook near

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.430
Melbourne and landing in Perth.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:06.320
Then, less than a month later, he already
had a new ambition – to be the first to

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.560
fly across the Tasman Sea to New Zealand.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.460
Not only would this have been another gratifying
feather in Smithy's aviator cap, but he hoped

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.410
that it would also secure an airmail contract
between the two countries.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:21.720
At the time, achieving the first trans-Tasman
flight was the "next big thing" for many Australian

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.160
and New Zealand aviators.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.120
However, in January of that year, two Kiwi
pilots named John Moncrieff and George Hood

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.800
disappeared over the sea while attempting
the flight.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.430
No trace of them or their plane, the Aotearoa,
has ever been found.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:38.180
Kingsford Smith's venture in September 1928
was the first attempt since their disappearance.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.180
Image suggestion: Southern Cross landing in
New Zealand

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.529
https://teara.govt.nz/en/photograph/7481/southern-cross-monoplane
He had a crew of four again.

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:44.360
Charles Ulm was by his side as relief pilot
and joining them were radio operator Thomas

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.580
McWilliams and navigator Harold Arthur Litchfield.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:50.380
They boarded the Southern Cross and took off
on September 10.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:55.350
After a flight of 14 hours and 25 minutes,
they landed at Wigram Airport in Christchurch.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.279
Tens of thousands of people were there to
cheer them on, including students and public

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:02.980
servants who were given the day off for this
momentous occasion.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:11.160
After the Highs Come the Lows
With another world first under his belt, Kingsford

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.810
Smith was the talk of the town once again,
but he would soon find himself involved in

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:20.560
a few controversies with fatal consequences
that had a lasting impact on his reputation.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.680
Now that the trans-Tasman flight was also
out of the way, Kingsford Smith's pragmatic

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:26.180
side started showing again.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:30.610
In 1929, he intended to fly to England to
purchase four new aircraft for his budding

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:31.610
company.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.550
He took a crew with him aboard the Southern
Cross, but had to make an emergency landing

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.089
in the middle of nowhere in Western Australia.

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:41.360
The crew was safe, but the plane was in no
condition to fly, so they patiently waited

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:42.360
to be rescued.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.279
Of course, the disappearance of a hero the
stature of Charles Kingsford Smith prompted

00:12:46.279 --> 00:12:48.830
a massive search involving many other pilots.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:52.020
Among them were two of his friends named Keith
Anderson and Bobby Hitchcock.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:56.230
They flew aboard the Kookaburra hoping to
spot a sign of the downed airplane.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:13:00.110
Unfortunately, they also suffered problems
and crash landed in the Tanami Desert.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:04.190
While Smithy and his crew would be found and
rescued, these two pilots died of exposure.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:07.149
Image suggestion: Kookaburra Memorial
It hardly seemed fair to blame Kingsford Smith

00:13:07.149 --> 00:13:11.770
for the tragedy, but rumors soon started circling
that the forced landing of the Southern Cross

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:13.610
had been nothing but a publicity stunt.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:17.410
The media sensed a juicy story and fanned
the flames of controversy.

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:21.779
Some even accused the aviator of purposely
making it harder for search teams to find

00:13:21.779 --> 00:13:22.779
them.

00:13:22.779 --> 00:13:25.620
That way, the longer they stayed gone, the
more press they received.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.790
The incident became known as the "Coffee Royal
Affair", named so after the black coffee and

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:34.390
brandy drink which the crew of the Southern
Cross purportedly drank while awaiting rescue.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:38.640
An official inquiry was launched into the
matter and, although Kingsford Smith was exonerated,

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.130
his reputation was tarnished in Australia.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:49.470
Australian National Airways Opens and Closes
Overseas, everyone still loved Smithy so he

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:54.430
climbed into his trusty Southern Cross and
took to the skies for a few more headline-grabbing

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:55.430
flights.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:59.940
In June 1930, he completed an east-west crossing
of the Atlantic from Ireland to America in

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:01.420
thirty-one-and-a-half hours.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:06.121
This also meant that he completed a circumnavigation
of the world which he started with his first

00:14:06.121 --> 00:14:07.121
trans-Pacific journey.

00:14:07.121 --> 00:14:10.830
Later that year, he won an air race from England
to Australia, breaking the speed record while

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:11.990
flying solo.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:17.360
Alongside Charles Ulm, in 1929 Smithy finally
founded the airline he dreamed of for years,

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.670
which was called Australian National Airways.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:25.260
It commenced operations in January 1930 with
five airplanes with Kingsford Smith himself

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:26.490
flying the Southern Cloud.

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:27.800
The company wasn't around for long.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:31.850
The airline had to close down following another
landmark moment in the history of aviation,

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:37.320
but one that Kingsford Smith could have certainly
done without – the first airliner to disappear

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:38.540
during a flight.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:42.960
On March 21, 1931, the Southern Cloud left
Sydney for Melbourne.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.010
Kingsford Smith was not the one flying.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:46.890
On board were two crewmen and six passengers.

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:51.870
They all perished in a crash in the Snowy
Mountains of New South Wales due to bad weather.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:56.750
Their final location remained a mystery for
27 years until a hiker named Tom Sonter stumbled

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:57.770
upon the wreckage.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:01.720
It goes to show that, back then, being a passenger
on an airplane took about as much courage

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.750
as being a pioneering aviator.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:04.750
Image suggestion: Southern Sun crash
https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-144699765/view

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.550
A few months later, the Southern Sun also
crashed while trying to make the first airmail

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.550
delivery from Australia to England.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:12.790
Charles Kingsford Smith spent all his time
and money convincing everyone that flying

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:18.000
was a safe and practical way to transport
mail and people in order to start a successful

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.000
airline.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.279
Once he finally launched his company, he showed
them the exact opposite.

00:15:22.279 --> 00:15:26.769
Back then, at least, flying was still anything
but safe.

00:15:26.769 --> 00:15:34.540
The Journey of the Southern Cross Minor
It seems like almost every other plane that

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:38.700
Kingsford Smith piloted apart from the Southern
Cross was destined for tragedy.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:44.600
In 1931, he added a new aircraft to his collection:
an Avro Avian Mk.V he dubbed the Southern

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:45.600
Cross Minor.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:50.190
He wanted to use it to set a new speed record
for the Australia-England flight, but was

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:51.190
unsuccessful.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:54.890
He sold it a few years later to another aviation
pioneer, British pilot Bill Lancaster.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:55.890
Image suggestion: Bill and Jessie
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Billandchubbie.jpg

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:58.050
Lancaster had a career worthy of mention.

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:01.769
It mirrored that of Kingsford Smith and was
highlighted by triumphs, controversies, and

00:16:01.769 --> 00:16:02.769
death.

00:16:02.769 --> 00:16:05.440
He also started flying during World War I
and later pursued it as a career.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:10.390
In 1927, he achieved one of the longest flights
in a small aircraft by journeying from England

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:11.390
to Australia.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:16.180
He was accompanied by his lover, Jessie "Chubbie"
Miller, who became a successful aviatrix in

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.180
her own right.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:19.750
A few years later, the two were living in
Miami, Florida.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:23.850
While Lancaster was in Mexico, looking for
work, they brought in a writer named Haden

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.650
Clarke to help Miller write her memoirs.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.730
They started an affair and Miller even decided
to leave Lancaster for her new beau.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:34.680
Upon hearing this, the aviator returned home
and, soon enough, Clarke was found dead from

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.209
a gunshot to the head.

00:16:36.209 --> 00:16:37.930
Lancaster was charged with murder.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.280
His alibi didn't hold water and he admitted
to forging two suicide notes.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.400
He had recently purchased a revolver and witnesses
heard him say that he will get rid of Clarke.

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:50.390
You would think this would be an open &amp; shut
case, but Lancaster was actually found "not

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:51.390
guilty".

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:55.370
He might have dodged prison, but his reputation
took a big hit and Lancaster became a pariah.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:59.709
He was desperate to regain just a fraction
of his lost prestige and that is where the

00:16:59.709 --> 00:17:01.339
Southern Cross Minor came in.

00:17:01.339 --> 00:17:06.149
Lancaster decided to go for the England-South
Africa speed record as that was the in vogue

00:17:06.149 --> 00:17:07.149
flight of the day.

00:17:07.149 --> 00:17:12.049
He bought the plane from Kingsford Smith and
set off on April 11, 1933.

00:17:12.049 --> 00:17:14.559
Everything that could go wrong did.

00:17:14.559 --> 00:17:17.449
Lancaster had gotten lost several times and
fell behind schedule.

00:17:17.449 --> 00:17:21.839
He was so sleep deprived that officials tried
to detain him when he landed for refueling.

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:25.429
He ignored them and pressed on and crashed
in the Sahara Desert.

00:17:25.429 --> 00:17:26.542
Image suggestion: Wreck of the Southern Cross
Minor

00:17:26.542 --> 00:17:28.720
Miraculously, the pilot escaped his ordeal
almost unscathed.

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:33.210
He still had a serious problem, though, as
now he was stranded in the middle of the desert

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.759
with few supplies.

00:17:34.759 --> 00:17:38.649
He waited to be rescued, occasionally firing
off flares, but the rescue never came.

00:17:38.649 --> 00:17:42.359
Lancaster died eight days later, on April
20, 1933.

00:17:42.359 --> 00:17:47.359
It was almost 30 years until French troops
found his mummified corpse and the wreck of

00:17:47.359 --> 00:17:48.359
the Southern Cross Minor.

00:17:48.359 --> 00:17:53.030
He had left behind a diary detailing his last
days on Earth which was later published with

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:54.240
Miller's permission.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.200
What's left of the plane sits in the Queensland
Museum.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:02.809
The Lady Southern Cross and Smithy's Final
Flight

00:18:02.809 --> 00:18:07.470
In 1935, Kingsford Smith had a very close
call in the Southern Cross while attempting

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:09.799
the first trans-Tasman airmail flight.

00:18:09.799 --> 00:18:12.940
One of the plane's three motors stopped due
to a damaged propeller blade.

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:17.289
The three men aboard the craft only survived
because Smithy's co-pilot, P.G. Taylor, climbed

00:18:17.289 --> 00:18:22.279
out of the cockpit and transferred oil from
the dead motor to the others using a suitcase

00:18:22.279 --> 00:18:23.489
and a thermos flask.

00:18:23.489 --> 00:18:27.929
Kingsford Smith had to dump all the mail and
extra cargo and barely made it back to Sydney.

00:18:27.929 --> 00:18:31.700
This was a sign that, perhaps, it was time
to retire the Southern Cross.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:35.830
Smithy purchased a new plane – a Lockheed
Altair called the Lady Southern Cross.

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:39.299
He sold his famous airplane to the Commonwealth
of Australia so it could be put on display

00:18:39.299 --> 00:18:40.299
in a museum.

00:18:40.299 --> 00:18:42.640
At first, the new aircraft appeared to be
a worthy replacement.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:46.429
With it, Kingsford Smith performed the first
eastward flight over the Pacific Ocean, going

00:18:46.429 --> 00:18:48.179
from Australia to the United States.

00:18:48.179 --> 00:18:53.230
In 1935, he decided to use it in order to
break the England-Australia speed record.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:55.580
He set off on November 6, 1935.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.779
With Smithy was co-pilot Tommy Pethybridge.

00:18:57.779 --> 00:19:02.409
His longtime flying partner, Charles Ulm,
had vanished and, presumably, died a year

00:19:02.409 --> 00:19:04.350
prior while flying over the Hawaiian islands.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:05.549
Image suggestion: Leg of Lady Southern Cross
https://collection.maas.museum/object/141688

00:19:05.549 --> 00:19:09.129
Unfortunately, this was a fate the two would
share because the Lady Southern Cross also

00:19:09.129 --> 00:19:10.530
disappeared over the ocean.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:15.029
On November 8, it took off from India and
was headed to Singapore, but was never seen

00:19:15.029 --> 00:19:16.029
again.

00:19:16.029 --> 00:19:19.539
It is presumed the plane crashed in the Andaman
Sea, off the coast of Burma, killing Charles

00:19:19.539 --> 00:19:21.200
Kingsford Smith and his co-pilot, Tommy Pethybridge.

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:25.080
A year and a half after the disappearance,
an undercarriage leg and wheel washed up ashore

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:30.190
on an island and remains the only part ever
recovered from the Lady Southern Cross.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:35.030
In recent times, filmmaker Damien Lay claimed
in 2009 to have found the wreckage of the

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:36.030
airplane.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:40.139
His proclamation has been met with much skepticism
from experts and aviation historians.

00:19:40.139 --> 00:19:45.049
He has not made the location public and, presumably,
is still trying to raise funds for a recovery

00:19:45.049 --> 00:19:59.679
expedition.

